+## 6. Validity, stopping and deviations
+
+- Finiteness: activations, logits and probe predictions on evaluable bits are checked for finiteness before any argmax, quantization or aggregation; a non-finite value stops the run, and nothing is decided.
+- Inputs shared by both claims (checkpoint, generator guard, pair set and screening corpus, labels, extraction): a hash mismatch or a failed check blocks both claims.
+- Claim A pipeline (Z<=6 and P2 heads): a hash mismatch blocks claim A.
+- Claim B checks; any failure makes claim B not confirmable, and the failing pair is not dropped:
+  - hash match of Q, H, Π, R_0..R_19, W and the P2/P4/P8 heads;
+  - the hook is applied exactly once per call, at the output of blocks[1];
+  - within the same call, the query rows and every non-targeted prefix row are bit-identical before and after the hook;
+  - no-hook run equals `level_logits` without hooks bit for bit;
+  - self-donor through the full donor-capture and indexing path (d := r), at the both-empty cells and at all 91 prefix positions, gives logits bit-identical to the clean run;
+  - a real, different donor writes exactly the expected value at a designated cell;
+  - write-back: for every patched cell, | ‖x_after − x_before‖ − ‖step‖ | <= 1e-4 + 1e-6 ‖x_before‖;
+  - high-variance invariance (float64, before write-back): max over cells of |W (Delta H Hᵀ)| <= 1e-8 ‖W‖ ‖Delta‖;
+  - shuffled direction (float64): the cosine between W u and Π(W Delta) >= 1 − 1e-8 for every cell with Π(W Delta) ≠ 0.
+  Target norms, actual norms, scale factors and the alignment of each condition's step with Delta are logged (diagnostics, not tests).
+- An internal orbit collision (§4.1) blocks both claims.
+- No optional stopping: N is fixed at 400 and is not changed after any TEST outcome; the run is analysed once.
+- Restartability: the runner checks every input hash before starting, has a single writer and an atomic final write, and resumes only with identical inputs, order and verified partial outputs.
+- Deviations: any departure from this protocol is logged in `DECISIONS.md` before outcomes are read where possible and reported; a method change after TEST outcomes have been seen makes the affected claim exploratory; later methodological changes require a new protocol version, and TEST data already seen are not reused for confirmation.
 
